"""VO beats for How to Buy a Beechcraft Bonanza A36 (Ep9, N1811Y, 1981 A36).
Same story cut as Ep7: cold open + title -> choose -> ladder -> shortlist -> the one we ran -> report sections as questions -> offer -> keep watching -> verdict.
Plain-language standard (Alex 2026-10-09). Numbers as words, no ellipses, sentences of about 20 words or fewer.
Every market figure is for Bonanzas / A36s only (single-engine pistons)."""
BEATS = [
("b01", "title", "This Bonanza has been for sale for almost six months. The seller has cut the price three times, by sixty-five thousand dollars. It still asks about sixty thousand more than NextPlane's estimate. This is How to Buy a Beechcraft Bonanza A36. One real airplane for sale right now, one real NextPlane report, and what we would offer."),
("b02", "problem", "Step one. Choose which one. The Bonanza is Beechcraft's single-engine family, built since the late nineteen-forties. The A36 is the six-seat version, with a straight tail and big double doors at the back. Pilots buy it to carry family and bags at real cruising speed. This week, NextPlane is tracking about sixty A36s for sale with a U S registration. About fifty-five show a price."),
("b03", "ladder", "The first question is which Bonanza, because the model and the year set the price. These figures are for Bonanzas only. When I say typical, I mean the middle: half ask more, half ask less. A typical V-tail, the V35B, asks about two hundred thirty thousand dollars. The shorter F33A, about two hundred sixty-five thousand. A36s built through nineteen eighty-three, about three hundred thousand. A36s from the mid eighties to the early nineties, about three hundred seventy thousand. The turbocharged B36TC, about four hundred twenty-five thousand. Newer A36s, about four hundred sixty-five thousand. And the current G36, about six hundred eighty thousand dollars."),
("b04", "shortlist", "Now narrow it down. Say you want an older A36, built through nineteen eighty-three. Twenty-nine of them show a price. Half ask between about two hundred seventy and three hundred twenty-five thousand dollars. Our airplane is the fourth most expensive of the twenty-nine. So the seller wants a premium. We ran the report to see if it earns one."),
("b05", "pick", "This one. A nineteen eighty-one A36, sold by a dealer in Tennessee, asking three hundred sixty thousand dollars. The airframe shows fifty-one hundred hours. That is right at the market average. The engine is the headline. It is a factory-remanufactured Continental with only nineteen hours on it. That leaves about seventeen hundred hours until it is due for an overhaul. And the panel is new Garmin glass, with a Garmin GFC five hundred autopilot."),
("b06", "header", "Step two. Evaluate the one. Open the NextPlane Aircraft Report. The top section is the summary, with warnings and good news in one place. No accident record. The same company has owned it for twenty-two years. No loan recorded against it in the last year. And one item flagged as critical: a mandatory software update for the autopilot."),
("b07", "value", "This section is the valuation, NextPlane's estimate of what a similar airplane is worth today. The estimate is about three hundred thousand dollars. This one asks about sixty thousand more. But the estimate makes no adjustment for the new engine, so we checked ourselves. A36s of this era with older engines ask a typical two hundred seventy-two thousand. A36s with engines under six hundred hours ask about three hundred four thousand. This one asks more than both groups."),
("b08", "engine", "So what is the extra money buying? This section compares hours with the market. The airframe is about average. The engine has about eight hundred eighty fewer hours than the typical A36 for sale. That matters, because an engine overhaul is a big bill. The report sets aside about thirty-five dollars for every hour you fly, toward the next overhaul. At that rate, the newer engine is worth roughly thirty-one thousand dollars. That explains about half of the sixty thousand. The rest is the panel and the autopilot. Decide what that equipment is worth to you, before you call."),
("b09", "sister", "Here is a comparison the report surfaced. The same dealer just listed another nineteen eighty-one A36. It has about twenty-one hundred fewer hours on the airframe. Its engine has two hundred ninety hours, and it has a similar Garmin panel. It asks three hundred seventy-five thousand, just fifteen thousand more. If you call about one, ask about both."),
("b10", "flight", "Is it flying? This section shows how much the airplane really flies, from NextPlane's flight data. Over two years, we see ninety-two flights and about thirty-nine hours. In the last twelve months, about thirty-one hours. In the twelve months before that, only about seven. Most of the recent flying came this spring and summer, in short local hops. That could fit a new engine being flown in, but it is only a guess. Ask when the engine went in, and see it in the logbook."),
("b11", "pricehist", "Has the seller moved? This section is the listing history, every price change NextPlane recorded. Listed in May at four hundred twenty-five thousand. Cut in June, July and September, sixty-five thousand in total. We followed forty-two A36s first listed this spring and summer. Twenty-four cut their price, by a typical seven percent. Twenty-three were still for sale after ninety days. This seller has cut about fifteen percent, roughly twice the typical. A36 sellers often wait months. A patient buyer has leverage."),
("b12", "owner", "Who owns it? This section covers ownership: the registered owner, how long they have held it, and any recorded loans. The owner is a Tennessee company, registered since October two thousand four. Long ownership is often a good sign: one owner, one maintenance story to follow. The records also show a loan release in September. That usually means an old loan was paid off. The seller is a dealer, not the owner. So ask for the release paperwork, and run a title search."),
("b13", "ads", "What should the logbooks show? This section lists mandatory safety fixes, called airworthiness directives. The report matches sixteen to this serial number. The one it flags is a Garmin directive to update the autopilot software. This airplane has that autopilot, so ask for the record. The report also builds a checklist of what the logbooks should already show. Bring that list to your pre-buy inspection."),
("b14", "sdr", "Has it ever been hurt? The listing reports no damage. NextPlane finds no accident record. Reported service problems: only twelve on the whole A36 fleet in five years. None were reported on this airplane."),
("b15", "opcost", "What will it cost to keep? This section estimates a year of ownership. At one hundred twenty hours a year, about thirty-two thousand dollars, or about two hundred sixty-six an hour. Fuel is the biggest line, about eleven thousand. Insurance is figured on the full asking price, so get a real quote."),
("b16", "seller", "And who am I dealing with? A dealer. NextPlane's dealer data shows its asking prices run about nine percent above market. Its airplanes take longer to sell than the same models elsewhere. So this dealer is used to waiting. Use that time to do your homework."),
("b17", "offer", "Step three. Price the offer. NextPlane's estimate is about three hundred thousand dollars. Add about thirty-one thousand for the newer engine. That is roughly three hundred thirty thousand before the panel. The sister ship with far fewer airframe hours asks three hundred seventy-five thousand. And this seller has already cut three times. An opening offer around three hundred thirty thousand dollars is reasonable. That is about eight percent under the ask, close to the typical A36 cut. Make it subject to a pre-buy inspection, the engine paperwork, the autopilot directive and a clean title."),
("b18", "watch", "Step four. Keep watching. In the last two weeks, four new A36s were listed. Sellers made nine price cuts, and no increases. Right now, A36 prices are moving down."),
("b19", "verdict", "So who is this airplane right for? A buyer who wants a six-seat Bonanza, no engine bill for years, and a modern Garmin panel. It is not right for you if you want the lowest price, or plan to install your own panel. Priced above similar airplanes, with a clean history. Check the engine paperwork, the loan release, and what the panel is worth to you."),
("b20", "end", "Before you call the broker, check the data at NextPlane dot U S."),
]
if __name__ == "__main__":
    import re
    n = sum(len(t.split()) for _, _, t in BEATS); print("words", n)
    assert not any("..." in t for _, _, t in BEATS)
    for b, _, t in BEATS:
        for s in re.split(r"(?<=[.?!])\s+", t):
            if len(s.split()) > 22: print("LONG", b, len(s.split()), s)
